# {{name}}

A Calimero app scaffolded with `cargo mero new`.

## Workflow

```
cargo mero build                    # compile -> wasm-opt -> embed ABI (res/{{name_snake}}.wasm)
cargo mero test                     # TestHost unit tests + convergence tests (no node needed)
cargo mero key generate -o key.json          # once: create your own Ed25519 signing key
cargo mero bundle --key key.json --no-icon   # build, write manifest.json, sign, tar dist/com.example.{{name}}-0.1.0.mpk
meroctl app install --path dist/com.example.{{name}}-0.1.0.mpk ...   # install on a node (merod)
```

Artifacts:
- `res/{{name_snake}}.wasm` - the built, ABI-embedded wasm
- `res/abi.json`, `res/state-schema.json` - emitted by `cargo mero build` (no `build.rs` needed)
- `dist/com.example.{{name}}-0.1.0.mpk` - the signed bundle

## Signing

Sign with a key you generate and keep private. `key.json` is git-ignored; back it
up, because a different key gives the bundle a different signer and app identity.

```
cargo mero key generate -o key.json           create your own Ed25519 key
cargo mero bundle --key key.json --no-icon    sign with it (CI: export MERO_SIGN_KEY=...)
```

For local testing only, `cargo mero bundle --dev --no-icon` signs with a
well-known public development key. Anyone can produce that signature and the
registry refuses such bundles, so do not use it for anything you ship.

## Where the code lives

- `src/lib.rs` - state, events, logic, and the `#[cfg(test)]` TestHost tests
- `tests/converge.rs` - multi-replica convergence test
- `GUIDE.md` - how an agent (or a person) uses the app; `cargo mero bundle` ships it as `metadata.guide`
- `Cargo.toml` - SDK pins, the `[package.metadata.calimero]` app id, and the
  `app-release` / `app-profiling` build profiles

Full docs: https://calimero-network.github.io/core/
